ans are  themselves why is that well they come  from the same communities basically the  Russians actually oftentimes will put  them back to where they were born or  where they were recruited from and they  know the people and so they know who the  insurgents are and if they can't find  the insurgents they know who the  families are so they know essentially  whom to threaten and who to actually go  into the villages and identify and say  you're an insurgent you're coming with  us  the Russians don't they typically don't  have the language skills most Russians  don't speak Chechen most Russian  soldiers are eighteen-year-old  conscripts they don't have much time in  there they're very poorly paid they  don't want to be there and so the  Russians when they come through  typically brutalize but brutalize on a  large scale and they don't actually know  who the insurgents are the Chechens when  they come through know whom to brutalize  essentially and so they come through and  they find the insurgents and they remove  them from the population and so over  time one of the main conclusions it as  the Russians sweep the villages they  come through and they do these  operations to pull the insurgents out  the violence goes up after the the  Russians leave when the Chechens of  comparable size and comparable level  brutality towards the population comes  through the violence drops dramatically  so we get a net reduction of about 40  percent subsequent levels of insurgents  once the Chechens are sweeping their own  people so that's one of the main  conclusions the second is that this is  an incredibly dangerous policy bgb  practicing because what you're  essentially doing is arming communities  within the community that you're trying  to control and setting them at odds  against one another and this has nothing  for the post-war stability so the  Russians have bought a measure of peace  right now by essentially turning the  Chechens against themselves once the  Russians withdr